1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,051
Lesson 3.9.

2
00:00:02,431 --> 00:00:04,897
Non-equilibrium conductance matrices.

3
00:00:06,017 --> 00:00:10,290
An open chemical reaction network,
fed by chemostats that fix

4
00:00:10,290 --> 00:00:14,073
some concentrations, looks
like an electrical circuit.

5
00:00:14,453 --> 00:00:19,323
Currents are reaction fluxes and voltages
are chemical-potential differences.

6
00:00:19,703 --> 00:00:23,267
Raux, Goupil and Verley showed
that this analogy can be

7
00:00:23,267 --> 00:00:26,831
made precise with non-equilibrium
conductance matrices.

8
00:00:27,211 --> 00:00:31,492
That way, a large network is analyzed
by connecting modules, like resistors.

9
00:00:32,617 --> 00:00:36,133
Take a reversible reaction
with mass-action kinetics.

10
00:00:36,513 --> 00:00:43,433
The current is the forward flux minus
the backward one, j = k⁺[A] − k⁻[B].

11
00:00:43,813 --> 00:00:48,062
The thermodynamic force is k_B T
times the log of the ratio of the

12
00:00:48,062 --> 00:00:51,990
two fluxes, which is minus the
reaction's free-energy change.

13
00:00:52,370 --> 00:00:55,950
Current and force always have
the same sign: their product

14
00:00:55,950 --> 00:00:59,098
is the entropy production,
which is never negative.

15
00:01:00,217 --> 00:01:07,178
We define the reaction's resistance as
the ratio r = f/j, just like Ohm's law.

16
00:01:07,558 --> 00:01:11,874
Near equilibrium, the
force is small and the

17
00:01:11,874 --> 00:01:16,386
current is proportional
to it: r ≈ k_B T/j_eq,

18
00:01:16,766 --> 00:01:21,931
where j_eq is the exchange flux, what
goes back and forth in each direction.

19
00:01:22,311 --> 00:01:26,696
A fast reaction conducts well; a
slow one is a large resistance.

20
00:01:27,076 --> 00:01:29,874
Far from equilibrium the
resistance is no longer

21
00:01:29,874 --> 00:01:32,498
constant: it depends on
the stationary state.

22
00:01:33,617 --> 00:01:37,065
Now let's connect two
reactions in series: A ⇄

23
00:01:37,065 --> 00:01:40,064
B ⇄ C, with A and C fixed by chemostats.

24
00:01:40,444 --> 00:01:43,485
In the stationary state the
concentration of B adjusts

25
00:01:43,485 --> 00:01:46,415
until the same current flows
through both reactions.

26
00:01:46,795 --> 00:01:51,158
The forces add, because chemical-potential
differences chain together.

27
00:01:51,538 --> 00:01:57,307
With the same current and forces
that add, the resistances add:

28
00:01:57,307 --> 00:02:02,161
R = r₁ + r₂, and the module's
conductance is G = 1/R.

29
00:02:02,541 --> 00:02:05,620
It is the resistance-addition
law, now for chemistry.

30
00:02:06,750 --> 00:02:09,327
In a network with several
cycles and several

31
00:02:09,327 --> 00:02:11,904
chemostats, the resistances
become matrices.

32
00:02:12,284 --> 00:02:16,794
Each cycle has its current,
and the cycle resistance

33
00:02:16,794 --> 00:02:20,611
matrix relates forces
and currents: F = R J.

34
00:02:20,991 --> 00:02:23,142
Its inverse is the conductance matrix.

35
00:02:23,522 --> 00:02:26,132
It is symmetric and
positive semi-definite,

36
00:02:26,132 --> 00:02:28,925
because entropy production
cannot be negative.

37
00:02:29,305 --> 00:02:33,528
When modules are connected in series,
their matrices combine like resistances,

38
00:02:33,528 --> 00:02:37,209
and the result matches the direct
calculation for the whole network.

39
00:02:38,317 --> 00:02:41,257
With all rate constants
equal to one and C fixed

40
00:02:41,257 --> 00:02:43,831
at one, let's vary the concentration of A.

41
00:02:44,211 --> 00:02:48,362
Near equilibrium, with A
at 1.1, the conductance is

42
00:02:48,362 --> 00:02:52,189
0.52, very close to the
linear value, one half.

43
00:02:52,569 --> 00:02:56,286
With A equal to five,
the current is two, the

44
00:02:56,286 --> 00:03:00,169
total force 1.6 k_B T
and the conductance 1.24.

45
00:03:00,549 --> 00:03:03,392
With A equal to a hundred,
the conductance exceeds ten.

46
00:03:03,772 --> 00:03:08,288
Far from equilibrium the network
conducts much more than linear response

47
00:03:08,288 --> 00:03:12,931
predicts, but resistance addition is
still exact at each stationary state.

48
00:03:14,050 --> 00:03:18,552
This circuit theory lets us analyze
large networks by modules, design

49
00:03:18,552 --> 00:03:23,251
chemical computers where currents and
forces are signals, and keep track

50
00:03:23,251 --> 00:03:27,492
of dissipation, which is a positive
quadratic form in the forces.

51
00:03:27,872 --> 00:03:30,748
In a lab challenge you
will compare a simulated

52
00:03:30,748 --> 00:03:34,296
stationary current with the
conductance-matrix prediction.

53
00:03:35,417 --> 00:03:40,165
Frequent mistakes: treating the resistance
as constant far from equilibrium.

54
00:03:40,545 --> 00:03:43,402
Adding forces of reactions
that are not in series.

55
00:03:43,782 --> 00:03:46,906
Forgetting that it is the
chemostats that fix the forces.

56
00:03:47,287 --> 00:03:50,890
And confusing the conductance,
current over force, with the

57
00:03:50,890 --> 00:03:54,311
differential conductance, the
derivative of the current.

58
00:03:55,417 --> 00:04:00,111
In summary: each reaction has
a force, k_B T times the log of

59
00:04:00,111 --> 00:04:04,190
the flux ratio, and a
resistance, force over current.

60
00:04:04,570 --> 00:04:08,785
In series, resistances add
exactly at the stationary state.

61
00:04:09,165 --> 00:04:13,079
In networks with several cycles,
symmetric positive conductance

62
00:04:13,079 --> 00:04:16,745
matrices generalize Ohm's law
to non-equilibrium chemistry.

63
00:04:17,850 --> 00:04:22,668
To practice: compute the force,
current and resistance of a reaction.

64
00:04:23,048 --> 00:04:24,632
Prove the linear limit.

65
00:04:25,012 --> 00:04:29,367
Find the chain's stationary state
and check resistance addition.

66
00:04:29,747 --> 00:04:33,918
And explain why the conductance
matrix is positive semi-definite.
